A near-miss pin (P[int], int is not a Stringer) coexists with a valid pin on the
same parameter (P[Name]) and a disjoint valid pin (Q[string]). The disjoint
Q[string] pin must merge into the valid Name cluster, not the poisoned int one, so
Make[Name, string]() serves both C and B internally and P[int] becomes an input.
The near-miss type int sorts before Name, so before validate-before-merge Q[string]
was greedily absorbed into the int cluster and silently demoted to a caller input;
now the wiring is invariant under the near-miss type’s sort order.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Stringer interface{ String() string }
type P[T any] struct{}
type Q[U any] struct{}
type S1 struct{}
type S2 struct{}
type S3 struct{}
type Name struct{}

func (Name) String() string { return "" }

//plumb:build
func Make[T Stringer, U any]() (P[T], Q[U]) { return P[T]{}, Q[U]{} }

//plumb:build
func A(p P[int]) *S1 { return nil }

//plumb:build
func B(q Q[string]) *S2 { return nil }

//plumb:build
func C(p P[Name]) *S3 { return nil }
